<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Đinh Liễn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">?–979</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Dinh Lien</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Đinh Liễn was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Đinh Tiên Hoàng</a>, the first emperor of the Đinh dynasty of Đại Cồ Việt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. He served his father as a commander and envoy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> court gave him the title of prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi">Giao Chỉ</a> in the 970s. In 979 he and his father were murdered by the official Đỗ Thích, a killing that opened the way for the seizure of power by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Hoàn</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinh-lien.jpg" alt="Đinh Liễn" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kien1980v at Vietnamese Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinhlien.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Đinh Liễn, also called Đinh Khuông Liễn, was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Đinh Bộ Lĩnh</a>, who reigned as Đinh Tiên Hoàng. He spent his childhood fighting for his father's cause during the Anarchy of the 12 Warlords. When he was a toddler his father's enemies held him hostage, and his father tried to kill him so that they would lose their leverage; Liễn later forgave him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When his father took the throne, Liễn received the title Nam Việt vương, king of Nam Việt. In 973 he was sent to Song China with tribute, and Song Taizu named him Grand Preceptor of Inspection, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">Jiedushi</a> of Tĩnh Hải quân and Governor of the Protectorate General to Pacify the South. In 975 the Song court raised him to Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi">Giao Chỉ</a>. The Song regarded him as the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, although his father held power in fact.</p><p>In 978 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Đinh Tiên Hoàng</a> named the four-year-old Đinh Hạng Lang crown prince, over the objections of officials. Liễn felt wronged by his father once more, and in the spring of 979 he sent his men to kill his half-brother, to the distress of his father and his father's wives. To console them he had masons carve the Uṣṇīṣa Vijaya Dhāraṇī Sūtra on 100 columns dedicated to the dead boy and other souls. Their remains, forgotten for almost 1,000 years, were found in 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Liễn never received the title of crown prince. In the winter of 979 his father was assassinated, and Liễn was killed as well when he came upon the fleeing assassin. Đỗ Thích, who had saved members of the Đinh family many times during the Anarchy of the 12 Warlords, was hastily executed by Nguyễn Bặc. Accusations were also made that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Hoàn</a> was involved in the killings, even that he planned the end of the Đinh dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Đinh Liễn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He sent tribute to the Song dynasty, which granted titles to his son <b>Dinh Lien</b> in the 970s, while using the title of emperor at home.</blockquote><a href="/state/dinh-dynasty">Dinh dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hoa Lư Ancient Capital, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.26,105.95&amp;year=979" aria-label="Hoa Lư Ancient Capital on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7406 1414 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Đinh Liễn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7856 1684h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7856 1684h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.26,105.95&amp;year=979">What was here in 979 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
